\Abstract{
We discuss some conceptual issues with the location shift parameterisation
underlying parameter and interval estimation in \code{wilcox.test},
especially for bounded or ordinal response variables.  Looking at other
\code{*.test} functions in package \pkg{stats}, we highlight established and
missing links between models and procedures and discuss why diagnostic tools
for model assessment and tools for power evaluation and sample size planning
have been lacking from \pkg{stats} so far.

Reiterating the, unfortunately not widely known, fact that the
Wilcoxon-Mann-Whitney Rank Sum statistic can be understood as the empirical
score statistic in a proportional odds model, we motivate alternative
parameterisations for the Wilcoxon and other linear rank tests where the
location shift is not defined on the scale of the response but on some
function of the distribution itself.  Many known and novel models,
parameters, and corresponding test and inference procedures arise, such as
log-hazard ratios in proportional hazard models, Lehmann alternatives, or a
generalised version of Cohen's $d$ corresponding to the van der Waerden
normal scores test.

A unified implementation for empirical maximum likelihood estimation and
inference in this framework is provided through the novel
\code{stats::free1way} function added to the \pkg{stats} package 
in \proglang{R} 4.6.0, along with functionality for model
diagnostics and power assessment.  The application of these tools is
described and illustrated by means of a series of novel analyses of data
originating from a randomised trial evaluating neck pain reduction by laser
therapy.

}

\IfFileExists{upquote.sty}{\usepackage{upquote}}{}
\begin{document}

%%
<<packages, echo = FALSE, results = hide, message = FALSE, warning = FALSE>>=
pkgs <- c("free1way.docreg", "ordinalCont", "lattice", "latticeExtra", 
          "MASS", "memisc", "survival", "bibtex")
OK <- sapply(pkgs, require, character.only = TRUE)
if (!all(OK)) {
    install.packages(pkgs[!OK])
    OK <- sapply(pkgs, require, character.only = TRUE)
    if (!all(OK))
        stop("package(s) ", paste(pkgs[!OK], collapse = ", "), " not available")
}
set.seed(290875)
options(prompt = "R> ", continue = "+  ", width = 70, 
        useFancyQuotes = FALSE, digits = 6)
@
%%

%%
<<tools, echo = FALSE>>=
## some helper functions
frmt1 <- function(x, digits = 1) {
    if (!is.numeric(x)) return(x)
    formatC(round(x, digits = digits), digits = digits, format = "f") 
}
frmt3 <- function(x) 
    frmt1(x, digits = 3)
frmtci <- function(x, digits = 3) {
    if (!is.numeric(x)) return(x)
    if (length(x) != 2) stop("not a confidence interval")
    return(paste("(", frmt1(x[1], digits = digits), 
                 ",", frmt1(x[2], digits = digits), ")"))
}
@
%%

%%
<<pain, echo = FALSE>>=
pain_df <- neck_pain
idt <- xtabs(~ id, data = pain_df)
miss <- names(idt)[idt < 3]
#pain_df <- subset(pain_df, !id %in% miss)
#pain_df$id <- factor(pain_df$id)
levels(pain_df$laser) <- c("Placebo", "Active")
levels(pain_df$time) <- c("Baseline", "7 weeks", "12 weeks")
pain <- rbind(subset(pain_df, laser == levels(pain_df$laser)[1]),
              subset(pain_df, laser == levels(pain_df$laser)[2]))
ties12 <- sum(duplicated(subset(pain, time == "12 weeks")$vas))
pain$vasB <- cut(pain$vas, breaks = c(-Inf, .6, Inf), 
                 ordered_result = FALSE, 
                 labels = c("None to moderate", "(Moderately) severe"))
cols <- trellis.par.get("superpose.line")$col[1:2]
names(cols) <- levels(pain$laser)
@
%%


\section{Introduction}

The history of statistical methodology over the last two centuries can be
told as a tale of powerful and ingeniously simple ideas being overshadowed
by simplistic yet computable, and thus practically applicable,
approximations.  The replacement of Laplace' least absolute differences by
Gau{\ss}' least squares buried median, or more generally, quantile and
distribution regression under the normal linear regression tombstone for
$150$ years \citep{Portnoy_1997}.  Fisher's definition of the likelihood as a quantity being
proportional to the \emph{probability} of the data under a given model
remains invisible when we introduce a log-likelihood as a log-density to
students.  Although the core ideas had been developed by Erich Lehmann, John
Tukey, George Box, and Sir David Cox during the two decades preceding
generalised linear models (GLMs), transformation models featuring
data-driven response transformations in addition to interpretable covariate
effects on latent scales never gained the same popularity as GLMs, maybe
except for the proportional hazards model and its replacement of the full by
the partial likelihood.  These three examples are just the tip of the
iceberg, and some parts of contemporary methodological research can be
understood as an attempt to excavate the origins.

This history still haunts us today, in research, teaching, and everyday
practice, and its fall-out is hard-wired into software, for example into the
\pkg{stats} package \citep{R}.  Focusing on the suite of classical tests, we hope that
diligent students first looking at the \pkg{stats} documentation will ask
questions like ``Why does \code{wilcox.test} report $p$-values and parameter
estimates when the latter are missing in \code{kruskal.test} and
\code{friedman.test}?'', ``Why are odds ratios in \code{mantelhaen.test}
only available for stratified $2 \times 2$ tables?'', ``Why can't
\code{wilcox.test} handle ordered or censored outcomes?'', ``Why are blocks
or strata not allowed in \code{wilcox.test} or \code{kruskal.test}?'', or
``Why is \code{power.wilcox.test} missing?''.

We provide a new look at many of the \code{*.test} functions in the
\pkg{stats} package through the lens of semiparametrically efficient
population- and permutation-based inference in distribution-free stratified
$K$-sample oneway layouts, whose core ideas are strongly connected to the
transformation models mentioned above.  The most prominent member of this
model family is the Wilcoxon-Mann-Whitney two-sample rank sum test
\citep[][we use the term ``Wilcoxon test'' hereafter]{Wilcoxon_1945,Mann_Whitney_1947}.  
We will concentrate on this
test for the comparison of two parallel groups, developed independently by a
number of researchers \citep{Kruskal1957}, and its implementation in
\code{wilcox.test} as our prime example and will provide explanations for
its limitations and introduce a more holistic and model-based understanding
aiming to address the conceptual and practical questions in a framework
including, but not being limited to, \code{wilcox.test}.  The new
\code{free1way} function, added to the \pkg{stats} package in \proglang{R}
4.6.0 published 2026-04-24, leverages modern computational tools for a
general and reasonably fast implementation of a broad model family.


\begin{figure}[ht!]
