The Wilcoxon test is often advertised as an nonparametric alternative to the
two-sample $t$-test.  In this line of thinking the replacement of confidence
intervals for the difference of means by confidence intervals for a location
shift seems to make sense.  In \proglang{R}, the Wilcoxon test is
implemented in \code{wilcox.test} with the option to compute confidence
intervals for such a parameter using the procedure proposed by
\cite{Bauer_1972} along with the Hodges-Lehmann estimate of a location
shift.  The authors of this article implemented these confidence intervals
early in 2000, and the method became publically available in
\code{wilcox.test} as part of \proglang{R} 1.1.0, published 2000-06-15.  The
impact of the authors' choice to implement confidence intervals for a
location shift in \code{wilcox.test} is visible in the citation history of
the original \cite{Bauer_1972} paper (Figure~\ref{fig:Bauer}).  All
$\Sexpr{pre2000}$ citations before 2000 were from methodological papers in
statistics journals.  The method was only applied in empirical research in
ecology, biochemistry, genetics, pharmacology, zoology and other fields
after the implementation in \code{wilcox.test} was published in \proglang{R}
1.1.0.

While the method and its implementation are technically correct, presenting
confidence intervals for a location shift in the output of
\code{wilcox.test} seems suboptimal in hindsight, because the Wilcoxon test
cannot be motivated as a test against shift alternatives \citep[][and
others]{Conroy_2012,Divine_Norton_Baron_2018,Fong_Huang_2019,Parker_Jernigan_Lansky_2020}. 
In fact, the Wilcoxon test is the nonparametric or empirical score test for
a log-odds ratio in a semiparametric transformation model.  Although the
connection between the Wilcoxon test and proportional odds models has been
discovered decades ago \citep{Whitehead1993} and is mentioned in prominent
textbooks \citep{Harrell2015RMS}, the details for absolutely continuous
outcomes have been worked out only more recently
\citep{Wang_Tian_2017,Liu_Shepherd_Li_2017}.  In this sense, the rank sum
statistic proposed by \cite{Wilcoxon_1945} in absence of an underlying
probabilistic model later turned out to coincide with the score statistic in
the well-defined proportional odds model, first described 35 years later
\citep[by][but yet without making the connection]{McCullagh_1980}.  In this
sense, the Wilcoxon test is not one of the simplistic approximations cited
above but the real thing.  However, with its implementation being
overshadowed by a simplistic location shift model, \code{wilcox.test} cannot
leverage the full capacity of the proportional odds model for estimation and
inference.  With a fully specified matching probabilistic model in place, it
makes sense to estimate the parameters of this model by optimising some
appropriate likelihood function followed by likelihood-based inference
procedures for the relevant parameters.  The novel \code{free1way} function
does exactly this, in a model family not only featuring log-odds ratios and
corresponding Wilcoxon tests, but also log-hazard ratios (Savage- or logrank
tests), log-reverse time hazard ratios \citep[tests against a Lehmann
alternative,][]{Lehmann:1953}, and a variant of Cohen's $d$ (van der Waerden
test), also in stratified designs.  In addition to parameter estimates,
tests, and confidence intervals in \code{free1way}, tools for model diagnostics by means of
probability-probability plots and for sample size planning became available
in \proglang{R} 4.6.0.

We proceed by introducing randomised clinical trial data we will use as a
motivation and for illustrating concepts and user interfaces throughout this
article.  Starting with the special case of proportional odds models, and
thus Wilcoxon tests, we explain how the model is parameterised and the core
concepts of maximum likelihood inference in this special case.  Following a
reanalysis of the clinical trial, we generalise the framework to other
treatment effects covered by \code{free1way}.


\section{Low-level Laser Therapy Against Neck Pain Study} \label{sec:neck}

\cite{chow_heller_2006} reported on a double-blind, randomised,
placebo-controlled superiority study evaluating the effect of 300 mW, 830 nm
laser on chronic neck pain for a total of $N = 90$ subjects at three time
points.  Pain was measured using a horizontal $10$cm Visual Analogue Scale
(VAS, mapped to the unit interval) before treatment start (``baseline''),
directly after $14$ treatments applied over the course of seven weeks (``7
weeks''), and four weeks after end of treatment (``12 weeks'').  The VAS
trajectories of all subjects are given in Figure~\ref{fig:parplot} and the
marginal distributions of VAS at the three time points are presented in
Figure~\ref{fig:ecdfplot}, separately for the two treatment arms (placebo
and active laser treatment).  The left panel in this figure highlights quite
substantial baseline differences and Table~\ref{tab:samp} indicates missing
values for the VAS measurements directly at the end of treatment.

\begin{table}[h]
%%
<<samplesize, results = tex, echo = FALSE>>=
toLatex(ftable(xtabs(~ laser + time, data = pain)))
@
%%
\caption{Low-level laser therapy against chronic neck pain
\citep{chow_heller_2006} data: Sample sizes in two treatment groups over
time. \label{tab:samp}}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[ht!]
%%
<<plot, echo = FALSE, width = 6, height = 4, fig = TRUE>>=
print(xyplot(vas ~ time | laser, data = pain, 
       groups = id, type = "b", col = rgb(.1, .1, .1, .1),
       lwd = 2, layout = c(2, 1),
       ylab = "Neck pain (on visual analogue scale)", 
       xlab = "Examinations"))
@
%%
\caption{Low-level laser therapy against chronic neck pain
\citep{chow_heller_2006} data: Joint distribution of pain measured on Visual
Analogue Scale for two treatment groups for three time points. \label{fig:parplot}}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[ht!]
%%
<<ecdf, echo = FALSE, width = 6, height = 4, fig = TRUE>>=
print(ecdfplot(~ vas | time, data = pain, groups = laser,
         xlab = "Neck pain (on visual analogue scale)", 
         panel = function(...) {
             panel.ecdfplot(...)
             panel.abline(a = 0, b = 1, col = "grey")
         },
         auto.key = list(columns = 2, position = "top")))
@
%%
\caption{Low-level laser therapy against chronic neck pain
\citep{chow_heller_2006} data: Marginal distributions of pain measured on Visual
Analogue Scale for two treatment groups for three time points. \label{fig:ecdfplot}}
\end{figure}


In their ``Statistical Methods'' section, \cite{chow_heller_2006} first
reflect on whether or not VAS should be treated as ordinal or as being
interval-scaled.  Techniques under both paradigms were applied, first
comparing change scores (VAS at 12 weeks minus VAS at baseline) using the
two-sample $t$-test and comparing an ordinal assessment of change in pain
severity using a proportional odds model.  Down the road, the methodological
issues arising in this trial motivated the development of ordinal regression
models for continuous scales \citep{manuguerra_heller_2010} by one of the
trial statisticians.

In addition to the analyses presented by \cite{chow_heller_2006}, one could
have asked the question ``Are the two marginal distributions of VAS four
weeks after end of treatment (third panel in Figure~\ref{fig:ecdfplot})
identical?''.  Because of the boundedness and lack of symmetry, at least in
the active laser group, of VAS recordings, many data analysts may opt for a
``nonparametric'' approach, and thus \code{wilcox.test} may seem an
attractive choice.  Running the following code in \proglang{R} 4.6.0 or
later results in
%
%%
<<wilcox.test, echo = TRUE>>=
(w <- wilcox.test(vas ~ laser, data = pain, subset = time == "12 weeks",
                  exact = TRUE, conf.int = TRUE))
@
%%
%
Prior to version \proglang{R} 4.6.0, \code{wilcox.test} 
would have refused to report an exact $p$-value citing the
presence of $\Sexpr{ties12}$ tied response values.  Even without such a
warning, the results are puzzling: We picked \code{wilcox.test} because of
its ``nonparametric'' nature, so where does the location shift parameter
come from?  Was VAS treated as an interval-scaled variable?  Where do then
the ties come from and how do they impact model and estimation?  The
documentation in \Rcmd{help("wilcox.test")} provides some clues: With
$\Prob(\text{VAS} \le \ry \mid \text{Placebo}) = F_\text{VAS}(\ry)$ the
marginal distribution of VAS in the placebo group, the results suggest a
marginal distribution of VAS in those treated with an active laser
$F_\text{VAS}(\ry - \beta)$, with $\hat{\beta} = \Sexpr{frmt3(w$estimate)}$
and a $\Sexpr{frmt1(attr(w$conf.int, "conf.level") * 100)}$\% confidence
interval $\Sexpr{frmtci(w$conf.int)}$.  The distribution function
$F_\text{VAS}$ is not specified, so the procedure comes with a
distribution-free flavour.  However, the model assumes that VAS is a
continuous variable, because for ordinal variables the expression
$F_\text{VAS}(\ry - \beta)$ is ill-defined.  Furthermore, the bounds of VAS
are not respected, because \eg~by definition $F_\text{VAS}(\ry - 0.22) = 0$
for all $\ry < 0.22$.  One could try to work around this problem by
analysing a transformation of VAS, maybe a probit transformation
$\Phi^{-1}(\text{VAS})$, but the location shift on this scale is not
directly interpretable.  Answers regarding the measurement scale of the
response, however, are much harder to find, mainly due to a lack of a model
motivating the test statistic.

This gap was filled by \cite{Wang_Tian_2017}, who proved that the Wilcoxon
test is a score test in a proportional odds model where the cumulative
distribution functions in the two treatment groups are parameterised as
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\Prob(\text{VAS} \le \ry \mid \text{Placebo}) & = & \expit(\h(\ry)) \\
\Prob(\text{VAS} \le \ry \mid \text{Active}) & = & \expit(\h(\ry) - \eshiftparm)
\end{eqnarray*}
%%
with $\expit(\rz) = (1 + \exp(-\rz))^{-1}$ denoting the distribution function of the
standard logistic distribution.
Consequently, \cite{Wang_Tian_2017} suggest to estimate log-odds ratios by
means of this model and to provide corresponding confidence intervals. 
Before we present the theoretical arguments, we follow their advice and
analyse the data by means of a Wald test and confidence interval obtained
from a proportional odds model.  \code{MASS::polr} \citep{MASS} implements
this model, however, only for ordered categorical response variables. 
Simply treating the observed VAS values as ordered categories leads to
%%
<<polr, message = FALSE>>=
w12 <- subset(pain, time == "12 weeks")
w12$vasE <- ordered(w12$vas)
p <- polr(vasE ~ laser, data = w12, Hess = TRUE)
summary(p)$coefficients["laserActive",]
confint(p)
@
%%
with log-odds ratio $\hat{\eshiftparm} = \Sexpr{frmt3(coef(p))}$ indicating
that pain was lower under active laser treatment because the negative shift
associates negative values of $\eshiftparm$ with smaller VAS values in the
treated group.  This log-odds ratio effect is invariant with respect to
monotone transformations of the response.  Unfortunately, the Wald $p$-value
is not identical to the $p$-value reported by \code{wilcox.test}, not even
when we use an asymptotic $p$-value instead of an exact one
%%
<<wpolr, message = FALSE>>=
wilcox.test(vas ~ laser, data = pain, subset = time == "12 weeks",
            exact = FALSE)$p.value
@
%%
In addition, the tests' $p$-value and the Wald confidence intervals are not congruent
%%
<<wpolrci, message = FALSE>>=
confint(p, level = 1 - w$p.value)
@
%%
and it can happen that \code{wilcox.test} suggests to reject equality of the
distribution functions at nominal level $\alpha$ yet a two-sided $(1 -
\alpha) \times 100\%$ Wald confidence interval includes zero or vice versa.

\section{Theory}

We first review the relevant theory of score tests (also known as Rao score
and Lagrange Multiplier tests) and corresponding confidence intervals in
Section~\ref{sec:scores}.  The Wilcoxon test is then embedded in this theory
as the nonparametric or empirical score test in a proportional odds model in
Section~\ref{sec:wilcoxon} and we apply this result to the construction of
confidence intervals in Section~\ref{sec:intervals}.  We generalise the
framework to stratified $K$-sample oneway layouts with $K > 2$ and describe the main
ideas underlying semiparametrically efficient population and permutation
inference in this model class in Section~\ref{sec:general}.

\subsection{Score Tests and Intervals} \label{sec:scores}

Consider a single parameter of interest $\eshiftparm \in \RR$ and other
parameters $\parm \in \RR^\dimparm$ and $N$ independent observations with
log-likelihood contributions $\ell_i: \RR^{1 + \dimparm} \rightarrow \RR$. 
The log-likelihood is given by $\ell(\eshiftparm, \parm) = \sum_{i = 1}^N
\ell_i(\eshiftparm, \parm)$ and its score function by
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mS(\eshiftparm, \parm) =
  \frac{\partial \ell(\eshiftparm, \parm)}{
        \partial (\eshiftparm, \parm^\top)^\top} = 
  (S_\eshiftparm(\eshiftparm, \parm), 
   \mS_{\parm}(\eshiftparm, \parm)^\top)^\top \in \RR^{1 + \dimparm}.
\end{eqnarray*}
%%
The first element of the gradient is the score with respect to the parameter
of interest $\eshiftparm$.  The observed Fisher information matrix for all
parameters $(\eshiftparm, \parm^\top)^\top$ is
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\FI(\eshiftparm, \parm) = 
  \frac{\partial^2 \ell(\eshiftparm, \parm)}{
        \partial^2 (\eshiftparm, \parm^\top)^\top} = 
\left( \begin{array}{ll}
    \eFI(\eshiftparm, \parm)_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm} & 
        \FI(\eshiftparm, \parm)_{\eshiftparm, \parm} \\
    \FI(\eshiftparm, \parm)^\top_{\eshiftparm, \parm} & 
        \FI(\eshiftparm, \parm)_{\parm, \parm} \\
\end{array} \right) \in \RR^{1 + \dimparm, 1 + \dimparm}
\end{eqnarray*}
with inverse
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mA(\eshiftparm, \parm) = \FI(\eshiftparm, \parm)^{-1} = 
\left( \begin{array}{ll}
    A(\eshiftparm, \parm)_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm} & 
        \mA(\eshiftparm, \parm)_{\eshiftparm, \parm} \\
    \mA(\eshiftparm, \parm)^\top_{\eshiftparm, \parm} & 
        \mA(\eshiftparm, \parm)_{\parm, \parm} \\
\end{array} \right) \in \RR^{1 + \dimparm, 1 + \dimparm}.
\end{eqnarray*}
%%
Both matrices are partitioned such that the first row and column position
corresponds to the parameter of interest $\eshiftparm$.  Under the composite
null hypothesis $H_0: \eshiftparm = \eshiftparm_0$ the ``nuisance''
parameters $\parm$ are unknown and the restricted maximum likelihood
estimator
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0) = \argmax_{\parm \in \RR^\dimparm}
\ell(\eshiftparm_0, \parm)
\end{eqnarray*}
%%
satisfies $\mS_{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0)) = \nullvec$. 
The quadratic score statistic simplifies to 
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
& & \mS(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))^\top \FI(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))^{-1} 
    \mS(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0)) \\
& & \quad = (S_\eshiftparm(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0)), \nullvec^\top)
      \FI(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))^{-1} 
      (S_\eshiftparm(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0)), \nullvec^\top)^\top \\
& & \quad = (\FI(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))^{-1})_{11} S_\eshiftparm(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))^2 \\
& & \quad =   A(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm}
  S_\eshiftparm(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))^2
\end{eqnarray*}
%
as explained for example in \citet[Formula~1.18]{Rao_2005}, \citet[pages
539--541]{Lehmann_1998}, \citet[example 8.11]{Pawitan_2001}, or
\citet[Formula~4.1.2]{Fahrmeir_Tutz_2001}, and we define the linear score
statistic for $H_0: \eshiftparm = \eshiftparm_0$ as
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
S(\eshiftparm_0) := A(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm}^{\nicefrac{1}{2}}
  S_\eshiftparm(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0)) .
\end{eqnarray*}
%
The ancillary statistic $S(\eshiftparm_0)$ can be used to construct $(1 - 2
\alpha) \times 100 \%$ score intervals $\{\eshiftparm_0 \in \RR \mid
Q(\alpha) < S(\eshiftparm_0) \le Q(1 - \alpha)\}$ where the quantiles are
such that $\Prob_{\eshiftparm}(S(\eshiftparm) \le Q(\alpha)) \le \alpha$ and
$\Prob_{\eshiftparm}(S(\eshiftparm) > Q(1 - \alpha)) \ge 1 - \alpha$ for
some $0 < \alpha < \nicefrac{1}{2}$ and $\eshiftparm$ denoting the true
unknown parameter of the data generating process. Asymptotically and under
suitable regularity conditions, $S(\eshiftparm) / \sqrt{N}
\stackrel{d}{\rightarrow} \ND(0, 1)$ and normal quantiles $Q(\alpha) =
\sqrt{N} \Phi^{-1}(\alpha)$ can be used to compute score confidence
intervals. Alternatively, in the $K$-sample situation we are concerned with,
under the null hypothesis $H_0:
\eshiftparm = 0$ stating equivalence of outcome distributions, 
the term $A(0, \hat{\parm}(0))_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm}$
is invariant with respect to permutations, and thus the exact permutation
distribution of $S_\eshiftparm(0, \hat{\parm}(0))$, or a Monte-Carlo
approximation thereof, can be used to define $Q(\alpha)$. Thus, 
exact or approximate conditional tests and confidence intervals based on
quantiles of the permutation distribution are available to complement the unconditional
asymptotic variants.

Score intervals are invariant with respect to monotone transformations and
computationally attractive because only the distribution of $S(0)$ under
$\eshiftparm_0 = 0$ needs to be computed.  However, computation of
$S(\eshiftparm_0)$ for $\eshiftparm_0 \neq 0$ requires updating the maximum
likelihood estimators $\hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0)$.

An equivalent formulation of score tests and confidence intervals for
$\eshiftparm$ via the profile log-likelihood 
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\tilde{\ell}(\eshiftparm) = \sum_{i = 1}^N \ell_i(\eshiftparm, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm))
\end{eqnarray*}
%%
is theoretically and computationally attractive.  In the semiparametric
models outlined in Section~\ref{sec:general}, the maximum likelihood
estimator $\hat{\eshiftparm}$ is asymptotically normal and efficient
\citep{MurphyVaart2000} and, because the log-likelihood $\ell$ and thus the
profile log-likelihood $\tilde{\ell}$ are concave functions of
$\eshiftparm$, confidence regions are convex sets \citep[Lemma
A.1.,][]{MurphyVaart1997}. For scalar parameters $\eshiftparm$, these
results ensure that we can compute congruent score or likelihood-ratio intervals by
inversion of the corresponding tests, that is, $(1 - 2\alpha) \times 100\%$ 
confidence intervals will exclude $\eshiftparm_0$ iff 
the hypothesis $H_0: \eshiftparm = \eshiftparm_0$ is rejected at level $2
\alpha$. In Appendix~\ref{app:edge}, we discuss situations where the log-likelihood
is very flat and thus the computation of parameter estimates and
corresponding confidence intervals fails, despite these theoretical results.

%This can be avoided by using a 
%first order Taylor approximation of $S$ around $\hat{\eshiftparm}$ (which
%ignores the terms involving $\hat{\parm}^\prime(\eshiftparm_0)$) 
%\begin{eqnarray*}
%S(\eshiftparm_0) & = & A(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm}^{\nicefrac{1}{2}} 
%  S_\eshiftparm(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))\\
%& \approx &  
%  A(\hat{\eshiftparm}, \hat{\parm})_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm}^{\nicefrac{1}{2}} 
