\subsection{The Wilcoxon Score Test} \label{sec:wilcoxon}

We will now discuss a model, featuring nuisance parameters $\parm$ in
addition to a certain parameter of interest $\eshiftparm$, whose likelihood
function gives rise to a score statistic $S(\eshiftparm_0)$ which, under the
null hypothesis $\eshiftparm = \eshiftparm_0 = 0$, is a linear function of
the Wilcoxon Mann-Whitney two-sample rank sum statistic.  Based on this
model, we will explain how to interpret the parameter $\eshiftparm$, how to
estimate it using maximum likelihood, and how to obtain confidence
intervals.  Furthermore, the model allows to identify situations where the
test will have high power, and situations where we cannot expect the test to
work well, demystifying the notion of its ``nonparametric'' nature.  The
model will serve as a blueprint for the development of more general models
for $K > 2$ samples in stratified designs in Section~\ref{sec:general}.

For a univariate at least ordered response $\rY \in \samY$ and $X \in \RR$
a numeric covariate, with $X \in \{0, 1\}$ being an appropriate contrast in
the two-sample situation, we study the proportional odds model for the
conditional distribution of $\rY$ given $X = x$
%%
\begin{eqnarray} \label{fm:polr}
\Prob(\rY \le \ry \mid X = x) = \expit(\h(\ry \mid \parm) - \eshiftparm x) =: F_\rY(\ry \mid x).
\end{eqnarray}
%%
The model features a logit-link ($\expit = \logit^{-1}$) and $\h(\cdot \mid \parm):
\samY \rightarrow \RR$, an unknown monotonically increasing transformation
function depending on parameters $\parm \in \RR^\dimparm$, and defines the
effect of a one-unit increase in $x$ by the log-odds ratio $\eshiftparm$
such that
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\Prob(\rY \le \ry \mid X = x + 1)}{
\Prob(\rY > \ry \mid X = x + 1)} = \exp(-\eshiftparm) \frac{\Prob(\rY \le \ry \mid X = x)}{
\Prob(\rY > \ry \mid X = x)} \quad \forall \ry \in \samY.
\end{eqnarray*}
%%
In the two-sample case, the model states that the odds of observing a value
of at most $\ry$ in the second sample ($x = 1$) is $\exp(-\eshiftparm)$
times the odds of observing a value of at most $\ry$ in the first sample ($x
= 0$).  The proportionality assumption ensures that the factor
$\exp(-\eshiftparm)$ applies to all possible cut-off values $\ry$. 
Equivalently, one can think of a series of binary logistic regression models
with different cut-off points $\ry$ for dichotomising the response $\rY \le
\ry$.  The corresponding intercepts increase as $\ry$ increases but the
log-odds ratio $-\eshiftparm$ remains constant.  It is well known
\citep{Prentice_1978} that the Wilcoxon test is the locally most powerful
rank test against shift-alternatives in the logistic model $\Prob(\rY \le
\ry \mid X = x) = \expit(\ry - \eshiftparm x)$.  The shift parameter
$\eshiftparm$ is equivalent to a log-odds ratio in this logistic model.  For
absolutely continuous responses, model (\ref{fm:polr}) applies a
transformation $\h(\rY \mid \parm)$ such that $\h(\rY \mid \parm) -
\eshiftparm x$ follows a standard logistic distribution.  The shift
parameter $\eshiftparm$ is no longer interpretable as a difference on the
original measurement scale of $\rY$ but the interpretation as log-odds ratio
remains valid.

In the binary logistic regression point of view, the parameters $\parm$ of
the monotonically increasing transformation function $\h$ are the increasing
intercepts.  Testing $H_0: \eshiftparm = 0$ in the two-sample situation is
equivalent to the null $\rY \perp \!\!\!  \perp X$ or $F_\rY(\ry \mid 0) =
F_\rY(\ry \mid 1)$ for all $\ry \in \samY$.  Our arguments rely on the fact
that the Wilcoxon test is the nonparametric or empirical score test for
$\eshiftparm_0$ in the proportional odds model (\ref{fm:polr}) because the
score statistic $S(0)$ is permutation-equivalent to the sum of the ranks in
the second group ($x_i = 1$).  \cite{Wang_Tian_2017} demonstrate that the
score function $S_\eshiftparm(0, \hat{\parm}(0))$ is a linear function of
the Wilcoxon statistic in the two-sample case, similar arguments are given
in the Appendix to \cite{Liu_Shepherd_Li_2017}.

To derive the score statistic $S(0)$ for model (\ref{fm:polr}), consider a
sample of $N$ independent observations $(\ry_i, x_i), i = 1, \dots, N$ with
$C \le N$ distinct ordered outcomes $\upsilon_{1} < \dots < \upsilon_{C}$ in
$\ry_1, \dots, \ry_N$.  The $i$th observation contributes
%%
\begin{eqnarray} \label{fm:loglik}
\ell_i(\eshiftparm, \parm) = \log(\expit(\h(\ry_i \mid \parm) -
\eshiftparm x_i) - \expit(\h(\ry_i- \mid \parm) - \eshiftparm x_i))
\end{eqnarray}
%%
to the nonparametric or empirical log-likelihood function \citep{Owen_2001}
derived from model (\ref{fm:polr}).  Here, $\ry_i-$ is the largest distinct
ordered outcome smaller than $\ry_i$.  The transformation function $\h$ is
parameterised nonparametrically through parameters $\parm \in \RR^{C - 1}$,
more specifically through $\eparm_0 = -\infty < \eparm_1 < \dots <
\eparm_{C} = \infty$, as
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\h(\upsilon_c \mid \parm)  =  \eparm_c
\text{ and }
\h(\upsilon_c - \mid \parm)  =  \eparm_{c - 1},
\end{eqnarray*}
\ie~one parameter is assigned to each but the largest of the distinct
ordered outcomes.

Under $H_0: \eshiftparm = 0$, we assign upranks $R_1, \dots, R_N$ to $\ry_1,
\dots, \ry_N$, with $R_i$ being the number of observations not larger than
$\ry_i$.  The marginal distribution function is estimated by the
nonparametric or empirical maximum likelihood estimator $\hat{\Prob}(\rY \le
\ry_i) = \nicefrac{R_i}{N}$, \ie~we apply the empirical cumulative
distribution function.  This estimator is the restricted nonparametric
maximum likelihood estimator under $\eshiftparm = 0$.  We obtain $\h(\ry_i
\mid \hat{\parm}(0)) = \logit(\nicefrac{R_i}{N})$ and the score statistic is
then
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
S(0) & = & \sqrt{A(0, \hat{\parm}(0))_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm}} 
  \sum_{i = 1}^N
  \left. \frac{\partial \log(\expit(\h(\ry_i \mid \hat{\parm}(0)) - \eshiftparm x_i) - 
                             \expit(\h(\ry_i- \mid \hat{\parm}(0)) - \eshiftparm x_i))}{\partial \eshiftparm}
  \right|_{\eshiftparm = 0} \\
& = & \sqrt{A(0, \hat{\parm}(0))_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm}}
  \sum_{i = 1}^N
  \frac{\expit^\prime(\h(\ry_i \mid \hat{\parm}(0)) - 0 x_i) - 
        \expit^\prime(\h(\ry_i- \mid \hat{\parm}(0)) - 0 x_i)}{
        \expit(\h(\ry_i \mid \hat{\parm}(0)) - 0 x_i) - 
        \expit(\h(\ry_i- \mid \hat{\parm}(0)) - 0 x_i)} x_i.
\end{eqnarray*}
%%
With $\expit^\prime(z) = \expit(z)(1 - \expit(z))$, $\expit(\h(\ry_i \mid
\hat{\parm}(0)) - 0 x_i) = \nicefrac{R_i}{N}$ and $\expit(\h(\ry_i- \mid
\hat{\parm}(0)) - 0 x_i) = \nicefrac{R_i - t_i}{N}$ ($t_i$ being the number
of ties equal to $\ry_i$) this simplifies to
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
S(0) = \sqrt{A(0, \hat{\parm}(0))_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm}}\sum_{i = 1}^N \frac{N - 2 R_i + t_i}{N} x_i.
\end{eqnarray*}
%%
For the two-sample case ($x_i \in \{0, 1\}$) with $N_1 := \sum_{i = 1}^N
x_i$ and $N_0 := N - N_1$, we obtain
%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{N}{2} \sqrt{A(0, \hat{\parm}(0))_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm}}^{-1} S(0) 
& = & \frac{N}{2} \sum_{i: x_i = 1} \left(1 - \frac{2}{N}\left(R_i -\frac{t_i}{2}\right)\right) \\
& = & \frac{N}{2}\left(N_1 - \frac{2}{N_0 + N_1} \sum_{i: x_i = 1} \left(R_i - \frac{t_i - 1}{2} - \frac{1}{2} \right) \right)\\
& = & \frac{N_1}{2} + \frac{N_1 (N_0 + N_1)}{2} - \sum_{i: x_i = 1} \left(R_i - \frac{t_i - 1}{2}\right) \\
& = & \underbrace{\frac{N_0 N_1}{2} + \underbrace{\frac{N_1(N_1 + 1)}{2}  - \underbrace{\sum_{i: x_i = 1} \left(R_i - \frac{t_i -
1}{2}\right)}_{= W}}_{=  -U}}_{= -(U - \Ex(U))} \\
\end{eqnarray*}
%%
where $W$ is the Wilcoxon statistic, \ie~the sum of the mid-ranks $R_i -
(t_i - 1)/2$ in the sample with $x_i = 1$, and $U = W - N_1(N_1 + 1)/2$ is
the corresponding Mann-Whitney $U$-statistic with $\Ex(U) = N_0 N_1 / 2$
under $H_0$.  The latter statistic, named \Rcmd{W}, is returned by
\code{wilcox.test}, with $x_i = 1$ whenever observation $i$ belongs to the
first group in terms of the ordering of the levels of the corresponding
factor.  In contrast, \code{free1way} reports the standardised statistic $S(0)$ named \Rcmd{Z} based on
$x_i = 1$ when observation $i$ belongs to the second group (corresponding to
the default treatment contrasts in most models in \proglang{R}).

This relationship between the score statistic $S(0)$ of a proportional odds
model for at least ordered responses and the Wilcoxon statistic $W$ in the
two-sample situation is significant from a practical as well as a
theoretical point of view.  Practically, we can use this result for the
construction of score intervals for $\eshiftparm$ (see
Section~\ref{sec:intervals}).  Theoretically, we see that there is no
difference in the treatment of binary ($C = 2$), ordinal ($C < N$), and
continuous ($C = N$ in the absence and $C < N$ in the presence of ties)
responses and, in the latter case, ties do not require special treatment. 
Shift effects on the original response scale are not present in this model
and thus is seems suboptimal for \code{wilcox.test} to report corresponding
estimates and confidence intervals.  However, it is perfectly appropriate to
report the maximum likelihood estimator $\hat{\eshiftparm}$, interpretable
as log-odds ratio, and a corresponding confidence interval in addition to a
corresponding $p$-value; we explain details in Section~\ref{sec:intervals}. 
Furthermore, we can apply the arguments presented by \citet[Chapter
15.5]{vdVaart_1998} to conclude that the Wilcoxon test is the locally most
powerful rank test against proportional odds alternatives for any baseline
distribution $\Prob(\rY \le \ry \mid X = 0)$ in the very same way as the
log-rank (or Savage) test is the locally most powerful rank test against
proportional hazards alternatives, see \citet[Example 15.16]{vdVaart_1998}. 
In this sense, the Wilcoxon test is distribution-free with maximal, and even
optimal, power against proportional odds alternatives.  At the same time, it
also implies that the Wilcoxon test is inefficient in other situations, such
as against hazard ratio alternatives or in cases where the distribution
functions cross.  Thus, tools for diagnostic assessment of the proportional
odds assumption are important.

The unconditional exact or asymptotic score test based on this statistic is
commonly applied for computing $p$-values against the null $H_0: \eshiftparm
= 0$ and \code{wilcox.test} implements this test.  Asymptotic versions rely
on an approximation of the variance of $W$ and exact versions compute the
permutation distribution of $W$, including in the absence of ties where 
conditioning on the
observed mid-ranks leads to conditional or permutation score tests for $W$,
with exact and asymptotic variants now (since R~4.6.0) being available in
\code{wilcox.test}.  The conditional Wilcoxon test is especially attractive
for the analysis of ordered categorical responses: The likelihood
(\ref{fm:loglik}) is the likelihood of a proportional odds model for
categorical data and the Wilcoxon statistic based on mid-ranks is
proportional to the corresponding score statistic.  It seems therefore
overly restrictive for \code{wilcox.test} to not allow ordered categorical
responses.



\subsection{Nonparametric or Empirical Score Intervals for Log-odds Ratios} \label{sec:intervals}

To derive unconditional and conditional $(1 - 2 \alpha) \times 100\% $ score
confidence intervals for the log-odds ratio $\eshiftparm$, we obtain
corresponding quantiles $Q(\alpha)$ and $Q(1 - \alpha)$ for $S(0)$.  The
lower confidence limit is given by the root of $S(\eshiftparm_0) -
Q(\alpha)$ and the upper limit by the root of $S(\eshiftparm_0) - Q(1 -
\alpha)$, where
%
\begin{eqnarray*}
S(\eshiftparm_0) = \sqrt{A(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm}}
  \sum_{i = 1}^N
  \frac{\expit^\prime(\h(\ry_i \mid \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0)) - \eshiftparm_0 x_i) - 
        \expit^\prime(\h(\ry_i- \mid \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0)) - \eshiftparm_0 x_i)}{
        \expit(\h(\ry_i \mid \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0)) - \eshiftparm_0 x_i) - 
        \expit(\h(\ry_i- \mid \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0)) - \eshiftparm_0 x_i)}
        x_i.
\end{eqnarray*}
%
Evaluating the score function requires computation of the nonparametric
maximum likelihood estimator $\hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0)$ and corresponding
inverse Fisher information $A(\eshiftparm_0,
\hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm}$ under $H_0:
\eshiftparm = \eshiftparm_0$.  With the rows of the design matrices of the
proportional odds model being $\bar{\basisy}_i = (x_i, e_{C +
1}(\ry_i)^\top)^\top$ and $\ubar{\basisy}_i = (x_i, e_{C +
1}(\ry_i-)^\top)^\top$, where $e_{C + 1}(\upsilon_p)$ and $e_{C +
1}(\upsilon_p-)$ are unit vectors of length $C + 1$, and parameters
$\gammavec = (\eshiftparm_0, \eparm_0, \dots, \eparm_{C})^\top$ (remember
$\eparm_0 = -\infty$ and $\eparm_{C} = \infty$, so there are $1 + C - 1 =
\dimparm + 1$ unknown parameters) such that $\bar{\basisy}_i^\top \gammavec
= x_i \eshiftparm_0 + \upsilon_p$ and $\ubar{\basisy}_i^\top \gammavec = x_i
\eshiftparm_0 + \upsilon_{p - 1}$ if $\ry_i = \upsilon_p$, we obtain the
Fisher matrix as \citep[cf.~Formula~(4) in][]{HothornMoestBuehlmann2017}
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\FI(\eshiftparm_0, \parm) & = & 
\left( \begin{array}{ll}
    \eFI(\eshiftparm_0, \parm)_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm} & 
        \FI(\eshiftparm_0, \parm)_{\eshiftparm, \parm} \\
    \FI(\eshiftparm_0, \parm)^\top_{\eshiftparm, \parm} & 
        \FI(\eshiftparm_0, \parm)_{\parm, \parm} \\
\end{array} \right) \\
& = & 
\sum_{i = 1}^N 
- \left\{\frac{\expit^{\prime\prime}(\bar{\basisy}_i^\top \gammavec)\bar{\basisy}_i\bar{\basisy}_i^\top -
      \expit^{\prime\prime}(\ubar{\basisy}_i^\top \gammavec) \ubar{\basisy}_i \ubar{\basisy}_i^\top}
     {\expit(\bar{\basisy}_i^\top \gammavec) - \expit(\ubar{\basisy}_i^\top \gammavec)} \right. \\
& &  \left. \quad \quad -\frac{[\expit^\prime(\bar{\basisy}_i^\top \gammavec) \bar{\basisy}_i - 
       \expit^\prime(\ubar{\basisy}_i^\top \gammavec) \ubar{\basisy}_i] }
     {[\expit(\bar{\basisy}_i^\top \gammavec) - \expit(\ubar{\basisy}_i^\top
\gammavec)]^2} \times 
[\expit^\prime(\bar{\basisy}_i^\top \gammavec)\bar{\basisy}_i^\top - 
       \expit^\prime(\ubar{\basisy}_i^\top \gammavec) \ubar{\basisy}_i^\top]
\right\}. 
\end{eqnarray*}
%%
The Fisher information $A(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))$ for
$\eshiftparm_0$ for unknown $\parm$ is then given by the inverse of the
Schur complement $\eFI(\eshiftparm_0,
\hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm} - \FI(\eshiftparm_0,
\hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))_{\eshiftparm, \parm} \FI(\eshiftparm_0,
\hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))_{\parm, \parm}^{-1} \FI(\eshiftparm_0,
\hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))_{\eshiftparm, \parm}^\top$.  It should be noted,
however, that for finite sample sizes, $4
/ N^2 A(0, \hat{\parm}(0)_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm}$ is not the inverse variance of
$U - \Ex(U)$ under $H_0: \eshiftparm = 0$. In any case, the analytic
expression allows computation of standard errors for $\eshiftparm$ for both
estimated and assumed parameters, the latter option allowing the power curve
to be approximated for certain values of $\eshiftparm$ in the alternative.


