\subsection{Alternative Two-sample Parameterisation}

Sometimes the proportional odds model is parameterised differently in the
two-sample situation, most importantly in terms of the probabilistic index,
area under the ROC curve (AUC), or the C-index \citep[a broad
overview is provided by][]{Conroy_2012}.  For $\rY(0) \sim \rY \mid X = 0$ and $\rY(1) \sim
\rY \mid X = 1$ we have
%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\text{AUC} = \Prob(\rY(0) < \rY(1)) + \nicefrac{1}{2}\Prob(\rY(0) = \rY(1)).
\end{eqnarray*}
%
For $\eshiftparm = -\infty$ or $\infty$, the AUC is zero or one.  For
$\eshiftparm = 0$, the AUC is $\nicefrac{1}{2}$.  For all other values, the
AUC is a monotone transformation of the log-odds ratio
\citep{Fay_Malinovsky_2018}
%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\text{AUC}(\eshiftparm) = \frac{\exp(\eshiftparm)(\exp(\eshiftparm) - 1 - \eshiftparm)}{
      (\exp(\eshiftparm) - 1)^2}.
\end{eqnarray*}
%
Numerous authors proposed confidence intervals for this parameter
\citep[][and many others]{DeLong_DeLong_Clarke-Pearson_1988,
Newcombe_2006-1,Newcombe_2006-2,Qin_Zhou_2006,Qin_Hotilovac_2008,
Pauly_Asendorf_Konietschke_2016,Feng_Cortese_Baumgartner_2017,Fay_Malinovsky_2018,
Perme_Menevski_2019},
either with or without explicit reference to the connection with the
Wilcoxon test.  Because score intervals are invariant with respect to
monotone transformations, we can apply the novel score intervals for
log-odds ratios $\eshiftparm$ developed here directly for the computation of
confidence intervals for the AUC.  It must be noted, however, that the null
hypothesis $H_0: \text{AUC} = \nicefrac{1}{2}$ is not equivalent to the null
$H_0: \eshiftparm = 0$: The former allows for variance heterogeneity whereas
the latter assumes equal distributions \citep{Fay_Proschan_2010}.

\subsection{Generalisation} \label{sec:general}

The technical arguments presented in this section allow the generalisation
of model~(\ref{fm:polr}) to the comparison of $K > 2$ samples by introducing
additional parameters $\delta_k$, to stratified designs by introducing
stratum-specific parameters $\parm$, and to other forms of the alternative
by replacing the logit-link with some other link function.  We introduce the
model underlying \code{free1way} in this section.

We consider $K$ treatment groups $\rT \in \{1, \dots, K\}, K \ge 2$ for an
at least ordered outcome $Y \in \samY$ observed in stratum $\rS \in \{1,
\dots, B\}$ out of $B \ge 1$ blocks with conditional cumulative distribution
function (CDF) $F_Y(y \mid \rT = k, \rS = b) = \Prob(Y \le y \mid \rT = k,
\rS = b)$.  Detecting and describing differential distributions arising from
different treatments is our main objective.  We refer to the first treatment
$\rT = 1$ as ``control''.

With model function $m: [0,1] \times \RR \rightarrow [0,1]$
\citep[introduced by][describing the power of rank tests against
distributional alternatives]{Lehmann:1953}, we describe the conditional
distribution under treatment $k$ as a function of the conditional
distribution under control and a scalar parameter $\eshiftparm_k$:
%%
\begin{eqnarray} \label{fm:free1way}
F_\rY(y \mid \rT = k, \rS = b) = m(F_\rY(y \mid \rT = 1, \rS = b), \eshiftparm_k).
\end{eqnarray}
%%
The model is assumed to hold for all blocks $b = 1, \dots, B$, treatments $k
= 2, \dots, K$, and outcome values $y \in \samY$ based on parameters
$\eshiftparm_2, \dots, \eshiftparm_K \in \RR$.  For notational convenience,
we define $\eshiftparm_1 := 0$.

This model formulation gives rise to several specific models, for example,
$m_\text{L}(p, \eshiftparm) = p^{\exp(-\eshiftparm)}$ (Lehmann
alternatives), $m_\text{PH}(p, \eshiftparm) = 1 - (1 -
p)^{\exp(-\eshiftparm)}$ (proportional hazards), $m_\text{PO}(p,
\eshiftparm) = \text{expit}(\text{logit}(p) - \eshiftparm)$ (proportional
odds), or $m_\text{Cd}(p, \eshiftparm) = \Phi(\Phi^{-1}(p) - \eshiftparm)$
(generalised Cohen's $d$).  Instead of directly working with $m$, we
parameterise the model in terms of some absolute continuous CDF $F$ with
log-concave density $f = F^\prime$ and corresponding derivative $f^\prime$,
and quantile or link function $F^{-1}$.  The location model
%%
\begin{eqnarray} \label{model}
F_Y(y \mid  \rT = k, \rS = b) = F\left(F^{-1}\left(F_Y(y \mid \rT = 1, \rS = b)\right) - 
                                       \eshiftparm_k\right), \quad k = 2, \dots, K
\end{eqnarray}
%%
describes different distributions by means of shift parameter on a latent
scale defined by $F$.  The negative shift term ensures that positive values
of $\eshiftparm_k$ correspond to the situation of outcomes being
stochastically larger in group $k$ compared to control.  The shift
parameters are invariant with respect to monotone transformations of the
response values, that is, transforming the observations of all treatment
groups by the same function does not affect the values of $\eshiftparm_k$. 
The choice $F(z) = \exp(-\exp(-z))$ gives rise to $m_\text{L}$, $F(z) = 1 -
\exp(-\exp(z))$ corresponds to $m_\text{PH}$, $F = \text{expit}$ leads to
$m_\text{PO}$, and $F = \Phi$ results in $m_\text{Cd}$.  The choice of $F$
is made a priori and determines the interpretation of $\eshiftparm_k$.

We are interested in inference for $\eshiftparm_2, \dots, \eshiftparm_K$, in
terms of confidence intervals and hypothesis tests of the form
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
& & H_0: \eshiftparm_k - \mu_k = 0, \text{``two.sided''}, \quad k = 2, \dots, K, \\
& & H_0: \eshiftparm_k - \mu_k \ge 0, \text{``less''}, \quad k = K = 2, \\
& & H_0: \eshiftparm_k - \mu_k \le 0, \text{``greater''}, \quad k = K = 2,
\end{eqnarray*}
%%
with the latter two options only for the two-sample case ($K = 2$). The null
values $\mu_k, k = 2, \dots, K$ allow testing hypotheses about specific
values of the shift parameters (and can be specified as argument \Rcmd{mu} to
\code{free1way}).

As before, for an ordered categorical outcome $Y$ from sample space $\samY =
\{\upsilon_1 < \upsilon_2 < \cdots < \upsilon_C\}$, we parameterise the
model in terms of intercept ($\vartheta_\cdot$) and shift
($\eshiftparm_\cdot$) parameters \begin{eqnarray*} F_Y(\upsilon_c \mid \rT =
k, \rS = b) = F(\vartheta_{c,b} - \eshiftparm_k), \quad c = 1, \dots, C,
\end{eqnarray*} that is, we replace the transformed control outcome
$F^{-1}\left(F_Y(\upsilon_c \mid \rT = 1, \rS = b)\right) = \vartheta_{c,b}$
with a corresponding intercept parameter.  These $C - 1$ intercept
parameters are block-specific and monotone increasing $\vartheta_{0,b} =
-\infty < \vartheta_{1,b} < \cdots < \vartheta_{C,b} = \infty$ within each
block $b = 1, \dots, B$.  We collect all model parameters in a vector
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\thetavec = (\theta_1 & := & \eshiftparm_2, \\
               & \dots & , \\
               \theta_{K - 1} & := & \eshiftparm_K, \\
               \theta_{K} & := & \vartheta_{1,1}, \\
               \theta_{K + 1} & := & \vartheta_{2,1} > \vartheta_{1,1}, \\
               &  \dots, & \\
               \theta_{K + C - 2} & := & \vartheta_{C-1,1} > \vartheta_{C-2,1}, \\
               \theta_{K + C - 1} & := & \vartheta_{1,2}, \\
               & \dots &, \\
               \theta_{B (C - 1) + K - 1} & := & \vartheta_{C-1,B} >
               \vartheta_{C-2,B}).
\end{eqnarray*}
%%
If there is no observation for level $c$ in block $b$, the corresponding
parameter is not identified and removed from $\thetavec$ (exceptions apply
when penalised estimation is performed, see Appendix~\ref{app:Jeffrey}). 
The parameter space is defined by all parameter vectors $\thetavec$
satisfying the monotonicity of the intercept parameters.  Violations always
lead to the log-likelihood function being undefined and thus taking the
value $-\infty$.  For the $i$th observation $(y_i = \upsilon_c, \rt_i = k,
s_i = b)$ from block $b$ under treatment $k$, the log-likelihood
contribution is
%%
\begin{eqnarray} \label{fm:f1wll}
\log(\Prob(\upsilon_{c - 1} < Y \le \upsilon_c \mid \rT = k, \rS = b)) = \log(F(\vartheta_{c,b} - \eshiftparm_k) - F(\vartheta_{c - 1,b} - \eshiftparm_k))
\end{eqnarray}
%%
with $\upsilon_0 = -\infty$.  For an absolutely continuous outcome $Y \in
\RR$, we define $\upsilon_c := y_{(c)}$, the $c$th distinct ordered
observation in the sample.  The log-likelihood above is then the empirical
or nonparametric log-likelihood.  If observations were independently
right-censored, the contribution of the event $Y > \tilde{y}$ to the
log-likelihood is
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\log(\Prob(Y > \tilde{y} \mid \rT = k, \rS = b)) = \log(1 - F(\vartheta_{c - 1,b} - \eshiftparm_k))
\end{eqnarray*}
%%
where $\upsilon_{c - 1} = \max \{\upsilon \in \samY \mid \upsilon \le
\tilde{y}\}$, that is, observations right-censored between $\upsilon_{c -
1}$ and $\upsilon_c$ correspond to the parameter $\vartheta_{c - 1,b}$.

Maximising this form of the log-likelihood leads to semiparametrically
efficient estimators \citep[Chapter 15.5,][]{vdVaart1998}.  In this
framework, tests against deviations from the hypothesis $H_0$ above are
locally most powerful rank tests, for example against proportional odds ($F
= \text{expit})$ or proportional hazards alternatives \citep[$F(z) = 1 -
\exp(-\exp(z))$,][Example 15.16]{vdVaart1998}.

Based on the score function $S(\delta_2, \dots, \delta_K)$ and the Fisher
information $\eFI(\delta_2, \dots, \delta_K)$, which can be derived in the
exact same way as presented for the proportional odds model
(Section~\ref{sec:scores}), maximum likelihood estimates $\hat{\delta_2},
\dots, \hat{\delta}_K$ can be supplemented by Wald and likelihood ratio
tests and confidence intervals as well as likelihood- and permutation-based
Rao score tests and intervals.  The power function for the Wald test can be
evaluated relatively quickly by means of a small simulation for estimating
the inverse Fisher information $\eFI(\delta_2, \dots, \delta_K)$ for a given
alternative, sample size, and control distribution.

\section{Implementation} \label{sec:impl}

Maximum likelihood estimation and parameter inference for
model~(\ref{fm:free1way}) is implemented in \code{free1way} under the
following principles.  Data is represented in form of a $C \times K \times
B$ contingency table, whose element $(c, k, b)$ is the number of
observations with configuration $(y = y_c, \rt = k, s = b)$.  In the
presence of right-censoring, a fourth dimension is added ($C \times K \times
B \times 2)$ whose first $C \times K \times B$ table presents
right-censoring and the second table contains numbers of events.  The code,
roughly $2600$ lines, was written in \proglang{R} exclusively, only
importing functionality from \pkg{Matrix} \citep{Matrix}.

Lessons learned from existing implementations of some aspects of
model~(\ref{fm:free1way}) have been taken into account.  Because the
likelihood (\ref{fm:loglik}) for a nonparametric version of the
transformation function $\h$ is equivalent to the likelihood of a
proportional odds model for ordered categorical outcomes, the nonparametric
maximum likelihood estimators $\hat{\eshiftparm}$ and $\hat{\parm}$ along
with $A(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))_{\eshiftparm,
\eshiftparm}$ can be computed from \code{polr} or \code{tram::Polr}
\citep{HothornMoestBuehlmann2017,Hothorn_2020}, simply by fitting the model to ordered
categories obtained by representing a numeric response as an ordinal factor. 
The same likelihood is implemented for numeric responses in \code{rms::orm}
\citep{rms} which provides an implementation of the model for continuous
responses \citep{Liu_Shepherd_Li_2017}.  Only \code{Polr} allowed
stratification and permutation-based inference so far.

The restricted maximum likelihood estimator $\hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0)$ can
also be computed from these functions (using $\eshiftparm_0 x_i$ as an
offset), only the computation of $A(\eshiftparm_0,
\hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))_{\eshiftparm, \eshiftparm}$ is not commonly
implemented.  The term $\FI(\eshiftparm_0,
\hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))_{\eshiftparm, \parm} \FI(\eshiftparm_0,
\hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))_{\parm, \parm}^{-1} \FI(\eshiftparm_0,
\hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))_{\eshiftparm, \parm}^\top$ is relatively simple
to compute, because $\FI(\eshiftparm_0, \hat{\parm}(\eshiftparm_0))_{\parm,
\parm}$ is a tri-banded matrix.  When the number of distinct observations
$C$ is large, the corresponding number of $\parm$ parameters is, in the
worst case scenario, growing with $N$.  In contrast to all other
implementations, \code{orm} leveraged the tri-banded structure of the
Hessian and employs a Newton-Raphson algorithm where parameter updates for
tens of thousands of parameters can be computed very fast thanks to
corresponding solvers being available in \pkg{Matrix}.  The
\proglang{FORTRAN} code underlying \code{orm}, and the extensive evaluation
of unconstrained optimisation in this context by \cite{Harrell2024},
inspired much of the tailored optimisation procedures implemented in
\code{free1way}.  Appendices~\ref{app:vign} and \ref{app:optim} provide more
detail regarding these aspects.

\section{Pain Reduction using free1way}

Performing several potentially interesting analyses of the neck pain
randomised trial based on model~(\ref{fm:free1way}) by means of
\code{free1way}, we explain how this novel implementation can help to
address some methodological difficulties \citep[such as those raised
in][]{chow_heller_2006} and how unification of concepts and procedures
scattered across different \code{stats::*.test} functions previously is
achieved.

\subsection{Diagnostic Checking}

Before we use \code{free1way} to fit a proportional odds model corresponding
to the Wilcoxon test presented in Section~\ref{sec:neck}, we assess if the
data are in line with the proportional odds assumption in two ways.  The
simplest idea is to reformulate model~(\ref{fm:polr}) as
%%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\logit\left(\Prob(\rY \le \ry \mid X = x)\right) = \h(\ry \mid \parm) - \eshiftparm x
\end{eqnarray*}
%%
and check if the two empirical distribution functions fitted to VAS
separately in the two groups are roughly parallel on the logit-scale. 
Unlike plotting functionality for the ECDF in packages \pkg{stats} or
\pkg{latticeExtra}, the \code{plot} method for \Rcmd{survfit} objects from
the \pkg{survival} package \citep{survival1} allows plotting on
user-supplied ordinate scales and thus, because the Kaplan-Meier estimator
computed by \code{survival::survfit} is identical to the ECDF in the absence
of right-censoring, we can misuse this functionality for our purposes in
Figure~\ref{fig:diag1}.  The two resulting step functions are reasonably
parallel and thus assuming proportional odds is appropriate.  We come back
to diagnostic testing once we have the fitted model in place.

\begin{figure}[ht!]
%%
<<po, echo = TRUE, width = 5, height = 5, fig = TRUE>>=
w12 <- cbind(subset(pain, time == "12 weeks"), ev = TRUE)
plot(survfit(Surv(vas, ev) ~ laser, data = w12),
     conf.int = FALSE, mark.time = FALSE, 
     fun = function(p) qlogis(p, lower.tail = FALSE),
     col = cols, las = 1, main = "12 weeks",
     xlab = "Neck pain (on visual analogue scale)",
     ylab = "Distribution function on logit scale")
legend("bottomright", legend = levels(w12$laser), col = cols,
       lty = 1, bty = "n", title = "Empirical")
@
%%
\caption{Low-level laser therapy against chronic neck pain
\citep{chow_heller_2006} data: Check of proportional odds assumption. The
empirical cumulative distribution functions for both treatment groups are
plotted on the logit-scale.
\label{fig:diag1}}
\end{figure}


%%
<<free1waytmp, echo = FALSE>>=
f <- free1way(vas ~ laser, data = pain, subset = time == "12 weeks")
@
%%
We proceed by fitting the proportional odds model, that is, we estimate a
log-odds ratio $\delta = \delta_2$ in addition to $C - 1 =
\Sexpr{length(f$intercepts[[1L]])}$ increasing threshold parameters $\parm$
by maximising the empirical likelihood, requesting the exact permutation
distribution to be computed in addition
%%
<<free1way>>=
f <- free1way(vas ~ laser, data = pain, subset = time == "12 weeks", 
              exact = TRUE)
@
%%
We add a visualisation of these parameters to Figure~\ref{fig:diag1},
enriching this model-free plot with the two, now model-based yet
distribution-free or ``semi-parametric'', step-functions $\h(\ry \mid
\hat{\parm})$ for the placebo group and $\h(\ry \mid \hat{\parm}) -
\hat{\eshiftparm}$ for the active group in Figure~\ref{fig:model}.  The
model-free (solid) and model-based (dashed) lines, the latter featuring a
constant vertical shift $\hat{\delta}$, are almost perfectly in line and
thus, with any potential worries about model fit out of our way, we can
start interrogating the fitted model.

We first assess if the log-likelihood is identical to the log-likelihood
of the model estimated by \code{polr}
%%
<<loglik>>=
### polr
logLik(p)
### free1way
logLik(f)
### number of log-odds ratio + intercept parameters
length(coef(f)) + length(f$intercepts[[1L]])
@
%%
where \Rcmd{df} is the number of total parameters (log-odds ratio
$\eshiftparm$ and intercepts $\parm$) but not the degrees of freedom,
because of the monotonicity constraint on $\parm$.  We can also check if the
parameter estimates and the standard errors are reasonably close
%%
<<coef>>=
all.equal(coef(p), coef(f))
all.equal(p$zeta, f$intercepts[[1L]], check.attributes = FALSE)
all.equal(vcov(p)[names(coef(p)),names(coef(p)), drop = FALSE], 
          vcov(f), check.attributes = FALSE)
@
%%


\begin{figure}[ht!]
%%
<<fplot, echo = FALSE, width = 5, height = 5, fig = TRUE>>=
<<po>>
su <- sort(unique(w12$vas))
lines(su[-length(su)], f$intercepts[[1]], 
      type = "s", lty = 2, col = cols["Placebo"])
lines(su[-length(su)], f$intercepts[[1]] - coef(f), 
      type = "s", lty = 2, col = cols["Active"])
legend("topleft", legend = levels(w12$laser), col = cols, 
       lty = 2, bty = "n", title = "Model-based")
suq <- quantile(w12$vas, prob = 1:3 / 4)
out <- sapply(suq, function(q) {
    px <- f$intercepts[[1]][which.min((su - q)^2)]
    arrows(q, px, q, px - coef(f), code = 3, length = .1, angle = 45)
    text(q, px - coef(f) + .5, labels = expression(hat(delta)))
})
@
%%
\caption{Low-level laser therapy against chronic neck pain
\citep{chow_heller_2006} data: Check of proportional odds assumption with
empirical and model-based logit-transformed distribution functions. The
vertical distance between the model-based step-functions is constant at 
$\mid \hat{\eshiftparm} \mid = \Sexpr{frmt3(abs(coef(f)))}$.
\label{fig:model}}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Permutation Inference}

We compute an exact permutation $p$-value and corresponding permutation
confidence interval for $\delta$.  The exact conditional $p$-value testing
$H_0: \delta = 0$ is identical to the one reported by \code{wilcox.test} in
Section~\ref{sec:neck}, however, parameter estimate and confidence interval
are now reported for log-odds ratios
%%
<<free1wayperm>>=
(sf <- summary(f, test = "Permutation"))
coef(f)
confint(f, test = "Permutation")
@
%%
With a negative value of $\hat{\delta} = \Sexpr{frmt3(coef(f))}$ (which is
identical to the estimate $\hat{\delta} = \Sexpr{frmt3(coef(p))}$ provided
by \code{polr}), pain measured by VAS was lower in the active group compared
to the placebo group.  Because of the invariance of the maximum likelihood
estimator and the score interval with respect to monotone transformations,
we can report the score inference results for odds ratios
%%
<<free1waypermOR>>=
(OR <- exp(coef(f)))
(ciOR <- exp(confint(f, test = "Permutation")))
@
%%
concluding that the odds of observing pain on the VAS at most as large as
$\ry$ four weeks after the end of active laser treatment is about half as
large as the corresponding odds in the placebo group, with an uncertainty
ranging from a relative odds reduction between $\Sexpr{round(ciOR[1L] * 100)}\%$ 
and $\Sexpr{round(ciOR[2L] * 100)}\%$.

Closer to the ``Wilcoxon parameter'' $\Prob(\text{VAS}_\text{Placebo} <
\text{VAS}_\text{Active}) + \nicefrac{1}{2}\Prob(\text{VAS}_\text{Placebo} =
\text{VAS}_\text{Active})$ for a pair of patients sampled independently from
the two groups is the model-based probabilistic index (PI), also known as
the area under the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve (AUC),
again being a monotone transformation of $\delta$ and thus a permutation
score interval being available
%%
<<free1waypermAUC>>=
coef(f, what = "AUC")
confint(f, test = "Permutation", what = "AUC")
@
%%
Interestingly, not only the model-based AUC but also the model-based ROC
curve is a simple function of $\delta$ \citep[see][]{SewakHothorn2023},
opening up a second possibility for assessing the proportional odds
assumption.  We compare the probability-probability plot or empirical ROC
curve with the model-based ROC curve in Figure~\ref{fig:ROC} utilising the
novel \code{ppplot} function added to the \pkg{stats} package in
\proglang{R} 4.6.0.  The empirical and model-based
ROC curves are quite close, so no red flags regarding lack of fit of the
proportional odds model are raised by this additional diagnostic tool.

\begin{figure}[ht!]
%%
<<gof, width = 5, height = 5, fig = TRUE>>=
x <- subset(pain, subset = time == "12 weeks")
with(split(x$vas, x$laser), 
     ppplot(Active, Placebo, conf.level = .95, 
            main = "Visual Analogue Scale at 12 weeks"))
@
%%
\caption{Low-level laser therapy against chronic neck pain
\citep{chow_heller_2006} data: Probability-probability plot (dots) and
model-based receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve (lines, with
$95\%$ confidence band). \label{fig:ROC}}
\end{figure}

Another interesting estimand is the overlap coefficient, the integral over
the minimum of the two, in case of VAS Lebesque, densities
%%
<<free1waypermOVL>>=
coef(f, what = "OVL")
confint(f, test = "Permutation", what = "OVL")
@
%%
This measure is between zero, \ie~a complete separation of VAS in the two
groups, and one for identical VAS distributions.  The interesting feature
here is that we do not have to estimate the two densities directly, under
the proportional odd assumption \citep{SewakHothorn2023}.

By changing the confidence level to the $p$-value, we can check if the
$p$-values and confidence intervals are congruent, \ie~$\eshiftparm_0$ is
contained in an $(1 - \alpha) \times 100\%$ confidence interval iff the
$p$-value testing $H_0: \eshiftparm = \eshiftparm_0$ is smaller than the
nominal level $\alpha$. This works reasonably well for permutation
confidence intervals, where the upper bound of the $(1 - p\text{-value})
\times 100\% = \Sexpr{frmt3((1 - sf$p.value) * 100)}\%$ confidence interval
is close to zero
%%
<<free1waypermCI>>=
confint(f, level = 1 - sf$p.value, test = "Permutation")
@
%%
Computing tests and confidence intervals using identical inference
procedures in \code{free1way} ensures that conclusions about effects drawn
from tests are in line with effect sizes obtained from confidence intervals.

\subsection{Likelihood Inference}

For larger sample sizes or under the population model with a priori fix
groups, classical likelihood inference procedures can be performed by
changing the \Rcmd{test} argument in the \code{summary} and \code{coef}
methods.  Rao score tests and intervals are available from
%%
<<free1wayRao>>=
summary(f, test = "Rao")
confint(f, test = "Rao")
@
%%
likelihood-ratio tests and intervals from
%%
<<free1wayLRT>>=
summary(f, test = "LRT")
confint(f, test = "LRT")
@
%%
and Wald tests and intervals from
%%
<<free1wayWald>>=
summary(f, test = "Wald")
confint(f, test = "Wald")
@
%%
Asymptotically, these three procedures are known to provide the same
results, but finite sample differences can be quite substantial.  Only score
and likelihood ratio inference is invariant with respect to monotone
transformations, and should be applied when results are reported for
functions of $\delta$.

\subsection{Power and Sample Size}

Other researchers trying to replicate the above results in an independent
trial may ask what sample size would be needed to reproduce the same effect
size with a certain power.  The novel \code{power.free1way.test} function
implements the power function of the Wald test based on simulation-based
estimates of standard errors as a function of effect size (argument
\Rcmd{delta}), sample size in each group (argument \Rcmd{n}), and nominal
level $\alpha$ (argument \Rcmd{sig.level}).  Because these simulations can
be performed relatively quickly, the power function can be solved for the
sample size if the requested power is specified:
%%
<<power>>=
(pt <- power.free1way.test(delta = coef(f), power = .8, sig.level = .05))
@
%%
Because this result for the Wald test is not exact, study designers would
check if the power of the planned inference procedure (such as an asymptotic
permutation test) is in fact close to the power requested by performing a
simulation study directly estimating the power.  Function \code{rfree1way}
can be used to sample, by default continuous, response values under a given
model, in our case a proportional odds model with given log-odds ratio
%%
<<simulation, eval = FALSE>>=
ex <- expression({
    df <- rfree1way(n = pt$n, delta = pt[["log-odds ratio"]])
    summary(free1way(y ~ groups, data = df), 
            test = "Permutation")$p.value
})
pvals <- replicate(1000, eval(ex))
binom.test(x = sum(pvals < pt[["sig.level"]]), n = length(pvals), 
           p = pt[["power"]])$conf.int
@
%% Save CRAN checking time
\begin{Schunk}
\begin{Sinput}
R> ex <- expression({
+      df <- rfree1way(n = pt$n, delta = pt[["log-odds ratio"]])
+      summary(free1way(y ~ groups, data = df), 
+              test = "Permutation")$p.value
+  })
R> pvals <- replicate(1000, eval(ex))
R> binom.test(x = sum(pvals < pt[["sig.level"]]), n = length(pvals), 
+             p = pt[["power"]])$conf.int
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
[1] 0.788472 0.837664
attr(,"conf.level")
[1] 0.95
\end{Soutput}
\end{Schunk}
%%
which suggests that such a replication trial with substantially larger
sample size compared to the initial study would achieve the desired power. 
As a means to increase precision, \cite{chow_heller_2006} performed
covariate-adjustment for baseline VAS, and we will implement this idea using
stratification in the next section.

\subsection{Stratification}

Before we begin, we follow Section~2.10 in~\cite{chow_heller_2006} and
categorise VAS measurements at all three time points into an ordinal
variable at four levels of increasing pain
%%
<<vasO>>=
pain$vasO <- cut(pain$vas, breaks = c(-Inf, .3, .6, .8, Inf), 
                 ordered_result = TRUE, 
                 labels = c("None to mild", "Moderate", 
                            "Moderately severe", "Severe"))
@
%%
Baseline pain is then added as a stratum variable to all patients
%%
<<vasBL>>=
xB <- subset(pain, time == "Baseline")
pain$vasBL <- xB$vasO[match(pain$id, xB$id)]
class(pain$vasBL) <- "factor"
@
%%
We repeat the proportional odds model analysis now separately in the four
strata defined by ordinal baseline VAS assuming that the log-odds ratio is
the same in all four strata.  Unfortunately, the exact stratified permutation
distribution is not yet implemented, so we use a larger
number of random permutation in each stratum to approximate this
distribution allowing permutation-based inference for the common odds ratio
%%
<<free1wayS, message = FALSE>>=
fb <- free1way(vas ~ laser | vasBL, data = pain, 
               subset = time == "12 weeks", B = 10000)
summary(fb, test = "Permutation")
exp(coef(fb))
exp(confint(fb, test = "Permutation"))
@
%%
This stratified, or conditional, odds ratio estimate is smaller than the
marginal odds ratio and less variable, however, the two estimands are not
directly comparable because the model is noncollapsible.

Because the proportional odds model and its empirical likelihood describe
and handle continuous and ordinal responses in the very same way, we can
repeat the analysis with the response on the four pain levels
%%
<<free1wayO, message = FALSE>>=
fO <- free1way(vasO ~ laser | vasBL, data = pain, 
               subset = time == "12 weeks", B = 10000)
summary(fO, test = "Permutation")
exp(coef(fO))
exp(confint(fO, test = "Permutation"))
@
%%
The results are very close, especially when comparing the two confidence
intervals.  This might encourage the designers of the replication study to
simplify pain assessment by asking study participants if the pain they
suffer is \Sexpr{paste(paste0("``", levels(pain$vasO)[seq_len(nlevels(pain$vasO) - 1)], "''"), collapse = ",")}, or 
\Sexpr{paste0("``", levels(pain$vasO)[nlevels(pain$vasO)], "''")}
instead of going all-in with VAS examinations.  We might wonder what the
impact on the required sample size for a potential replication study might
be when using an ordinal response under stratification for ordinal baseline
pain.  We first estimate the pain distribution under placebo in the four
strata

%%
<<placebo>>=
(btab <- xtabs(~ vasO + vasBL, data = pain, 
               subset = laser == "Placebo" & time == "12 weeks")[,4:1])
colSums(btab)
(ptab <- prop.table(btab, margin = 2))
@
%%
Because only one patient with severe baseline pain remained at this pain
level, we remove the first stratum for the sample size planning.  The number
of patients in first pain levels at baseline are twice as large compared to
the number of baseline patients with moderately severe pain, we use the
latter stratum as the first one and double the sample size in the other two
strata (argument \Rcmd{strata\_ratio}).  Furthermore, we define the discrete
four point distribution of the pain response for the control group, so that
the power of the Wald test for ordinal responses will be approximated in
this call to \code{power.free1way.test}

%%
<<powerb>>=
(pbt <- power.free1way.test(prob = ptab[,-1], strata_ratio = c(2, 2),
                            delta = coef(fO), power = .8, 
                            sig.level = .05))
@
%%

Because the power function is highly discrete in small $n$, the requested
power cannot be achieved exactly.  In any case, the number of patients to be
enrolled in such a trial would be much smaller than the two times $n =
\Sexpr{pt[["n"]]}$ necessary in a trial without stratification.

\subsection{Analysing Binary Responses}

Although we saw that converting continuous VAS to an ordinal variable at
four levels did not change the results substantially going further analysing
VAS dichotomised as $\text{VAS} \le .6$ is probably overdoing it.  However,
for binary responses in a two-arm trial, the stratified proportional odds
model is identical to the model underlying the Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel test:

%%
<<mh>>=
mh <- free1way(vasB ~ laser | vasBL, data = pain, 
               subset = time == "12 weeks")
summary(mh, test = "Permutation")$p.value
xt <- xtabs(~ vasB + laser + vasBL, 
            data = pain, subset = time == "12 weeks")
mantelhaen.test(xt, correct = FALSE, exact = FALSE)$p.value
@
%%

Indeed, the asymptotic score permutation test coincides with the
Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel test uncorrected for small samples \citep[without
continuity correction, as demonstrated by][]{DayByar1979}.

The two estimates of the common odds ratio and their confidence intervals
are not identical, because the Mantel-Haenszel and conditional maximum
likelihood estimator \citep{MehtaPatel1985} reported by
\code{mantelhaen.test} are not identical to the maximum likelihood estimator
computed by \code{free1way}.

%%
<<mhOR>>=
### Mantel-Haenszel
mantelhaen.test(xt, correct = FALSE, exact = FALSE)$estimate
### Conditional ML
mantelhaen.test(xt, correct = FALSE, exact = TRUE)$estimate
### ML
exp(coef(mh))
@
%%

The latter estimator and corresponding inference is only valid when the
number of blocks is fixed and the sample size grows within each block.  For
many strata with low number of observations, with matched-pairs designs
being the most extreme example, this is typically not the case as the number
of blocks corresponds to the number of observations and thus sample size. 
In this case, the maximum likelihood estimator computed by \code{free1way}
is known to suffer finite sample bias and both the Mantel-Haenszel and
conditional maximum likelihood estimator correct this bias.  The phenomenon
also occurs for ordered categorical outcomes \citep{LiuAgresti1996}.  One
possible remedy to this problem is discussed in the next section and in
Appendix~\ref{app:Jeffrey}.

\subsection{Repeated Measures Analyses}

The analyses presented so far compare two conditional pain distributions
observed in the two treatment groups and thus, by definition, assess the
treatment effect for groups of patients.  It is hard to say something about
the pain reduction in single patients.  Therefore, in our final analysis, we
look at the study from a complete different angle, treating the three pain
measurements on the VAS as repeated measurements.  In the formulation of
model~(\ref{fm:free1way}), each patient is its own block or stratum.  This
is dangerous, because now the sample size within blocks is fixed (and equal
to one) whereas the number of blocks grows with number of patients.  For
ordinal outcomes like VAS, \cite{LiuAgresti1996} described the bias inherent
in the estimation procedure implemented in \code{free1way}.  We will now
discuss how we can still make use of \code{free1way} to analyse such
repeated measurement data.

We first look at the data from a slightly different perspective, by
comparing the individual pain measurements to baseline pain in
Figure~\ref{fig:base}.  Almost all patients under active laser treatment
reported a pain reduction after seven weeks.  In contrast, more than half
the number of patients in the placebo group reported more pain in this
period of time.  After 12 weeks, the pain reduction in the active group is
still substantial whereas the variability seemed to have increased in the
placebo group.

\begin{figure}[ht!]
\begin{center}
%%
<<indivplot, echo = FALSE, width = 5, height = 5, fig = TRUE>>=
pain$vasBLorg <- xB$vas[match(pain$id, xB$id)]
print(xyplot(vas ~ vasBLorg | time + laser, 
       data = subset(pain, time != "Baseline"),
       panel = function(x, y, ...) {
           panel.abline(a = 0, b = 1, col = "grey")
           panel.xyplot(x, y, ...)
           ltext(.1, .9, label = paste0(round(mean(y < x) * 100), "%"))
       }, xlab = "Baseline neck pain (on visual analogue scale)",
       ylab = "Neck pain (on visual analogue scale)"))
@
%%
\caption{Low-level laser therapy against chronic neck pain
\citep{chow_heller_2006} data: Comparison to follow-up pain measurements to
baseline pain in two treatment groups. The percentages refer to the fraction
of patients reporting an improvement (follow-up VAS $<$ baseline VAS).\label{fig:base}}
\end{center}
\end{figure}

We assume a patient-specific yet common log-odds ratio $\delta_2$ comparing
pain at the end of treatment with baseline and a common log-odds ratio
$\delta_3$ comparing the pain four weeks after end of treatment with
baseline for all patients in the placebo group.  In contrast to the model
employed before, the parameters $\delta_2$ and $\delta_3$ do not compare the
treatment-specific but the patient-specific distribution functions of VAS. 
Treating patients as blocks allows each patient to carry his or her own
baseline VAS distribution function, from which we only sample one
observation.  This also means that each patient comes with her or his own
VAS distribution at seven or $12$ weeks.  We make the strong assumption
that, for each patient, the follow-up VAS distributions are linked to the
baseline VAS distribution of this patient in terms of a proportional odds
model with, now common to all patients, log-odds ratio $\delta_2$ (seven
weeks) and $\delta_3$ ($12$ weeks).  In the same way, such effects also
exists in the active laser group (let's call them $\tilde{\delta}_2$ and
$\tilde{\delta}_3$), so we perform the analysis twice, starting with the
placebo group testing the hypothesis $H_0: \delta_2 = \delta_3 = 0$:
%%
<<friedmanP>>=
fP <- free1way(vas ~ time | id, data = pain, 
               subset = laser == "Placebo" & !(id %in% miss))
unlist(summary(fP, test = "Permutation")[c("statistic", "p.value")])
painP <- subset(pain, laser == "Placebo" & !(id %in% miss))
painP$id <- painP$id[,drop = TRUE]
unlist(friedman.test(vas ~ time | id, 
                     data = painP)[c("statistic", "p.value")])
@
%%
This gives exactly the same results as \code{friedman.test} when we remove
the observations with missing values from the data, because
\code{friedman.test} only works for full-factorial designs.  There is no
evidence against the null hypothesis here, so pain in patients in the
placebo group does not change over time.  Limiting this analysis to two time
points (baseline and 12 weeks) and VAS dichotomised at $.6$, the permutation
score test is identical to McNemar's test without continuity correction. 
The only relevant information here is the number of patients who switched
from VAS larger than $.6$ to VAS at most as large as $.6$ and vice versa
%%
<<mcnemar>>=
summary(free1way(vasB ~ time | id, data = pain, 
                 subset = laser == "Placebo" & time != "7 weeks"), 
        test = "Permutation")$p.value
xt <- xtabs(~ vasBL + vasB, data = pain, 
             subset = laser == "Placebo" & time != "7 weeks")
xt <- rbind(colSums(xt[1:2,]), colSums(xt[3:4,]))
mcnemar.test(xt, correct = FALSE)$p.value
@
%%

For the active laser group, the repeated measurements analysis leads to a
clear rejection of the null $H_0: \tilde{\delta}_2 = \tilde{\delta}_3 = 0$
for the response on the original VAS scale
%%
<<friedmanA>>=
fA <- free1way(vas ~ time | id, data = pain, 
               subset = laser == "Active" & !(id %in% miss))
unlist(summary(fA, test = "Permutation")[c("statistic", "p.value")])
painA <- subset(pain, laser == "Active" & !(id %in% miss))
painA$id <- painA$id[,drop = TRUE]
unlist(friedman.test(vas ~ time | id, 
                     data = painA)[c("statistic", "p.value")])
@
%%
but it remains unclear how large the trend to, hopefully (there is no
direction of effects in $p$-values), less pain actually is.  In addition,
this analysis does not contrast the temporal trend in the active laser group
with the trend in the placebo group.

The permutation $p$-values are valid, because they exclusively rely on
parameters estimated under the constraints formulated by the null hypotheses
($\delta_2 = \delta_3 = 0$) and thus the bias for these log-odds ratios
described by \cite{LiuAgresti1996} cannot occur.  Of course, fitting the
unrestricted model, parameter estimates for the log-odds ratios will be
biased and thus also confidence intervals will suffer from this problem.  We
can try to debias the maximum likelihood estimates, for example by adding a
penalty term to the log-likelihood function.  One possibility is to use an
idea similar to Firth's correction in generalised linear models
\citep{Firth1993,KosmidisFirth2020}, essentially penalising the
log-likelihood using a penalty term defined by the determinant of the Fisher
information matrix (also known as Jeffrey's prior), further details are
provided in Appendix~\ref{app:Jeffrey}.

We re-fit the two models to all patients treated in the two arms, compare
the parameter estimates and compute the contrast of interest for the
penalised parameters.  We start with the actively treated group
%%
<<MPL-A, eval = FALSE>>=
fA <- free1way(vas ~ time | id, data = pain, 
               subset = laser == "Active" & !(id %in% miss))
fAp <- free1way(vas ~ time | id, data = pain, 
                subset = laser == "Active" & !(id %in% miss), 
                MPL_Jeffreys = TRUE)
cbind(unpenalised = coef(fA), penalised = coef(fAp))
@
%%
\begin{Schunk}
\begin{Sinput}
R> fA <- free1way(vas ~ time | id, data = pain, 
+                 subset = laser == "Active" & !(id %in% miss))
R> fAp <- free1way(vas ~ time | id, data = pain, 
+                  subset = laser == "Active" & !(id %in% miss), 
+                  MPL_Jeffreys = TRUE)
R> cbind(unpenalised = coef(fA), penalised = coef(fAp))
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
             unpenalised penalised
time7 weeks     -5.53997  -1.63711
time12 weeks    -4.25218  -1.17997
\end{Soutput}
\end{Schunk}
%%
The log-odds ratios comparing the seven and $12$ weeks time points with
baseline are all negative, thus pain was reduced, however, more so after
seven weeks.  The penalised log-odds ratios are much smaller and reasonable.

For the placebo group, we obtain (with one warning indicating that the
optimiser tried to evaluate the likelihood~(\ref{fm:f1wll}) for a
configuration of the parameters violating the constraints)
%%
<<MPL-P, warning = FALSE, eval = FALSE>>=
fP <- free1way(vas ~ time | id, data = pain, 
               subset = laser == "Placebo" & !(id %in% miss))
fPp <- free1way(vas ~ time | id, data = pain, 
                subset = laser == "Placebo" & !(id %in% miss), 
                MPL_Jeffreys = TRUE)
cbind(unpenalised = coef(fP), penalised = coef(fPp))
@
%%
\begin{Schunk}
\begin{Sinput}
R> fP <- free1way(vas ~ time | id, data = pain, 
+                 subset = laser == "Placebo" & !(id %in% miss))
R> fPp <- free1way(vas ~ time | id, data = pain, 
+                  subset = laser == "Placebo" & !(id %in% miss), 
+                  MPL_Jeffreys = TRUE)
R> cbind(unpenalised = coef(fP), penalised = coef(fPp))
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
             unpenalised  penalised
time7 weeks     1.275001 -0.0137593
time12 weeks    0.653505 -0.3514360
\end{Soutput}
\end{Schunk}
%%
The penalised log-odds ratios clearly hint at a constant pain profile.

At the end of the day, we are interested in comparing the two treatment
groups with respect to the patient-specific log-odds ratios, that is, we
want to know if the pain reduction at the two time points was different
between the two groups, so we study the contrasts
%%
<<MPL-diff, eval = FALSE>>=
cbind(unpenalised = coef(fA) - coef(fP), 
      penalised = coef(fAp) - coef(fPp))
@
%%
\begin{Schunk}
\begin{Sinput}
R> cbind(unpenalised = coef(fA) - coef(fP), 
+        penalised = coef(fAp) - coef(fPp))
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
             unpenalised penalised
time7 weeks     -6.81497 -1.623354
time12 weeks    -4.90569 -0.828534
\end{Soutput}
\end{Schunk}
%%
The effect of penalisation is still clearly visible, as well as the
dampening of the treatment effect over time which can also be observed from
Figure~\ref{fig:base}.  It should be noted that the simultaneous comparison
of both time point to baseline gives different results compare to the
analysis of the data when we only compare a single time point to baseline,
because both shift parameters impact the intercept parameters, see also
Appendix~\ref{app:edge}.

\section{Odds and Ends}

The default link function \Rcmd{link = "logit"} implements the proportional
odds model.  Alternative link functions give rise to Lehmann alternatives
(\Rcmd{link = "loglog"}), proportional hazards models (\Rcmd{link =
"cloglog"}), and latent normal models with parameters comparable to Cohen's
$d$ (\Rcmd{link = "probit"}).  Independent right-censoring of response
values can be specified using \code{survival::Surv}.  It is important to
note that \code{free1way} always performs full maximum likelihood estimation
of all model parameters, so one must not assume to observe results identical
to other estimation techniques of the same model (most prominently, to the
partial likelihood for proportional hazards models in \code{survival::survfit}
or \code{survival::coxph}).  However, likelihood inference results for the
unstratified case should be identical to \code{orm} \citep{rms}.  This
highly tuned \proglang{FORTRAN} implementation served as a blue print for
many aspects of \code{free1way}.  For large samples with a limited number of
response categories, the computing times of the two implementations are
comparable.  For continuous responses and thus many threshold parameters
$\parm$, \code{free1way} is typically slower by a factor of three or four
compared to the lightning fast \code{orm}.

\section{Discussion}

The novel \code{free1way} function unifies many classical test procedures
available for decades in \pkg{stats}, among them \code{wilcox.test},
\code{kruskal.test}, \code{friedman.test}, \code{mantelhaen.test},
\code{prop.test}, and \code{mcnemar.test} and adds new members to this
family, most importantly distribution-free models against Lehmann
alternatives or Cohen's $d$.  The underlying models, corresponding
structural assumptions, and parameterisations surface, allowing data
analysts to perform diagnostic tests justifying (or not) the application of
such models.  Most importantly, \code{free1way} equips these classical and
novel models with a proper likelihood and corresponding parameter estimates
as well as a plethora of permutation- and population-based inference
procedures.  Power assessment and sample size planning is now directly
linked to estimation and inference procedures.

%% wilcox.test paired
%% Why stats? Passing on the flame, not worshiping the ashes.
%% Lacking: Interval censoring


%%
<<bib, echo = FALSE, results = hide, message = FALSE>>=
if (file.exists("REFERENCES.bib"))
    file.remove("REFERENCES.bib")
pkgs <- c("free1way.docreg", "tram")
bib <- do.call("c", lapply(pkgs, function(pkg) read.bib(package = pkg)))
bib <- bib[!duplicated(names(bib))]
if ("R" %in% names(bib))
    bib[["R"]] <- NULL
write.bib(c("rms", "tram", "MASS", "free1way.docreg", "Matrix", "survival"))
bib["Harrell2015RMS"]$doi <- gsub("_", "\\\\_", bib["Harrell2015RMS"]$doi)
write.bib(bib, file = "REFERENCES.bib")
R <- unclass(toBibtex(citation()))
R[[1]] <- "@Manual{R,"
cat(R, file = "Rpackages.bib", append = TRUE, sep = "\n")
@
%%
\bibliography{REFERENCES,Rpackages,WMW}

\begin{appendix}

\section{Implementation and Quality Assurance} \label{app:vign}

The \proglang{R} code implementing \code{free1way} is part of the
\pkg{stats} package.  The add-on package
\href{https://doi.org/10.32614/CRAN.package.free1way.docreg}{\pkg{free1way.docreg}}
\citep{free1way.docreg} serves as a vehicle for distributing
%%
\begin{itemize}
%%% direct link not allowed by R CMD check
\item A \href{https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=free1way.docreg}{literate
  programming} document linking theory and
  implementation and explaining all technical details.
The document is also available as package vignette
%%
<<vign, eval = FALSE>>=
vignette("free1way", package = "free1way.docreg")
@
%%
\item Additional \href{https://cran.r-project.org/web/checks/check_results_free1way.docreg.html}{regression tests}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Optimisation} \label{app:optim}

The choice of optimisation routines in \code{free1way} is in line with the
extensive experiments on this issue reported on by \cite{Harrell2024} and by
corresponding implementation in the \pkg{rms} add-on package \citep{rms}. 
Two aspects are of prime importance: First, it is not necessary to actively
perform constraint optimisation, because the log-likelihood
contributions~(\ref{fm:f1wll}) is only defined iff the intercept parameters
within each block are ordered.  Otherwise, the log-likelihood contribution
is \Rcmd{-Inf}. Thus, a parameter configuration is
inadmissible iff the log-likelihood is not finite and many optimisers can
handle this setup.

Second, the Fisher information is available in analytical form and the block
corresponding to the intercept parameters is tri-banded, facilitating very
efficient storage and parameter updates even when the number of distinct
outcomes and thus the number intercept terms is very large.

Two optimisation routines are currently in-built: \code{nlminb} and a
tailored version of Newton-Raphson with step-halving, the latter suggested
by \cite{Harrell2024}.  The main difference is that \code{nlminb} can only
handle Fisher information matrices stored in dense form whereas the latter
(internal) \code{.NewtonRaphson} function leverages the sparsity through
functionality offered by the \pkg{Matrix} \citep{Matrix} add-on package. 
For small sample sizes (and penalised estimation), the default is
\code{nlminb}, for all other problems \code{.NewtonRaphson} is applied. 
Users can switch between the two by \Rcmd{dooptim = "nlminb"} or
\Rcmd{dooptim = ".NewtonRaphson"}.

It is also possible to apply third-party optimisers, the only condition
being that a user-interface compatible with the one sported by \code{nlminb}
is available.  We illustrate ways to experiment with other optimisers using
the following problematic model
%%
<<Jeffrey-nlminb, warning = FALSE, eval = FALSE>>=
fPp <- free1way(vas ~ time | id, data = pain, 
                subset = laser == "Placebo", 
                MPL_Jeffreys = TRUE, logdet = TRUE)
@
%%
\begin{Schunk}
\begin{Sinput}
R> fPp <- free1way(vas ~ time | id, data = pain, 
+                  subset = laser == "Placebo", 
+                  MPL_Jeffreys = TRUE, logdet = TRUE)
\end{Sinput}
\end{Schunk}
%%
issuing the warning \Rcmd{unsuccessful optimisation in free1way: false convergence
(8)}.

We can implement the penalised negative log-likelihood
function~(\ref{fm:f1wpll}) for all model parameters $\theta = (\delta_2,
\dots, \delta_K, \parm)$ and compare the two penalised log-likelihoods
%%
<<Jeffrey-chk, eval = FALSE>>=
pll <- function(theta) {
    ret <- fPp$profile(theta, fix = seq_along(theta))
    ret$value - .5 * c(ret$logdet)
}
logLik(fPp)
-pll(fPp$par)
@
%%
\begin{Schunk}
\begin{Sinput}
R> pll <- function(theta) {
+      ret <- fPp$profile(theta, fix = seq_along(theta))
+      ret$value - .5 * c(ret$logdet)
+  }
R> logLik(fPp)
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
[1] -107.716
\end{Soutput}
\begin{Sinput}
R> -pll(fPp$par)
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
[1] -107.716
\end{Soutput}
\end{Schunk}
%%
Using the estimated parameters as starting values, we can refit the model
using Nelder-Mead
%%
<<Jeffrey-optim, eval = FALSE>>=
op <- optim(fPp$par, fn = pll)
logLik(fPp)
-op$value
coef(fPp)
op$par[1:2]
@
%%
\begin{Schunk}
\begin{Sinput}
R> op <- optim(fPp$par, fn = pll)
R> logLik(fPp)
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
[1] -107.716
\end{Soutput}
\begin{Sinput}
R> -op$value
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
[1] -107.511
\end{Soutput}
\begin{Sinput}
R> coef(fPp)
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
 time7 weeks time12 weeks 
  -0.0279659   -0.4194244 
\end{Soutput}
\begin{Sinput}
R> op$par[1:2]
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
[1] -0.0234542 -0.4213366
\end{Soutput}
\end{Schunk}
%%
and obtain a slightly larger value of the penalised log-likelihood but the
log-odds ratios are quite similar.  We can also write a wrapper around
\code{optim} and specify the name of this function as argument
\Rcmd{dooptim}, together with a named list of control arguments (which must
feature \Rcmd{trace} and \Rcmd{iter.max})
%%
<<Jeffrey-dooptim, warning = FALSE, eval = FALSE>>=
optNM <- function(start, objective, control = list(trace = FALSE), ...) {
    ctrl <- list(trace = control$trace)
    ret <- optim(start, fn = objective, control = ctrl)
    ret$objective <- ret$value
    ret
}
fPpNM <- free1way(vas ~ time | id, data = pain, dooptim = "optNM",
    subset = laser == "Placebo", 
    control = list(optNM = list(trace = FALSE, iter.max = 100)),
    MPL_Jeffreys = TRUE, logdet = TRUE)
logLik(fPp)
logLik(fPpNM)
@
%%
\begin{Schunk}
\begin{Sinput}
R> optNM <- function(start, objective, control = list(trace = FALSE), ...) {
+      ctrl <- list(trace = control$trace)
+      ret <- optim(start, fn = objective, control = ctrl)
+      ret$objective <- ret$value
+      ret
+  }
R> fPpNM <- free1way(vas ~ time | id, data = pain, dooptim = "optNM",
+      subset = laser == "Placebo", 
+      control = list(optNM = list(trace = FALSE, iter.max = 100)),
+      MPL_Jeffreys = TRUE, logdet = TRUE)
R> logLik(fPp)
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
[1] -107.716
\end{Soutput}
\begin{Sinput}
R> logLik(fPpNM)
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
[1] -124.206
\end{Soutput}
\end{Schunk}
%%
This exercise fails with a reportedly unsuccessful optimisation, and it
seems Nelder-Mead was not able reproduce its better result when using the
default starting values.

\section{Penalisation} \label{app:Jeffrey}

The sum $\ell$ of the log-likelihood contributions~(\ref{fm:f1wll}) is
penalised by a factor $\nicefrac{1}{2} A$ of the log-determinant of the
Fisher information
%%
\begin{eqnarray} \label{fm:f1wpll}
\ell(\delta_2, \dots, \delta_K, \parm) + \frac{1}{2} A \log(\det(\FI(\delta_2, \dots, \delta_K, \parm))),
\end{eqnarray}
%%
where the argument \Rcmd{MPL\_Jeffreys = }$A$ uses $A =$\Rcmd{FALSE} by default.

For binary outcomes and two time points, the penalised log-odds ratio is
identical to the one obtained by Firth's method in a binary logistic
regression model and close to the Mantel-Haenszel estimate
%%
<<binJeffrey, warning = FALSE, eval = FALSE>>=
library("brglm2")
xt <- xtabs(~ vasB + time + id, data = pain, 
            subset = laser == "Placebo" & time != "7 weeks",
            drop.unused.levels = TRUE)
dxt <- as.data.frame(xt)
Firth <- glm(vasB ~ 0 + id + time, 
             data = dxt, weights = dxt$Freq, 
             family = binomial(link = "logit"), 
             method = "brglmFit", type = "MPL_Jeffreys")
exp(coef(Firth)["time12 weeks"])
exp(coef(fJ <- free1way(xt, MPL_Jeffreys = TRUE)))
mantelhaen.test(xt)$estimate
### biased ML, the default
exp(coef(free1way(xt, MPL_Jeffreys = FALSE)))
@
%%
\begin{Schunk}
\begin{Sinput}
R> library("brglm2")
R> xt <- xtabs(~ vasB + time + id, data = pain, 
+              subset = laser == "Placebo" & time != "7 weeks",
+              drop.unused.levels = TRUE)
R> dxt <- as.data.frame(xt)
R> Firth <- glm(vasB ~ 0 + id + time, 
+               data = dxt, weights = dxt$Freq, 
+               family = binomial(link = "logit"), 
+               method = "brglmFit", type = "MPL_Jeffreys")
R> exp(coef(Firth)["time12 weeks"])
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
time12 weeks 
     1.12907 
\end{Soutput}
\begin{Sinput}
R> exp(coef(fJ <- free1way(xt, MPL_Jeffreys = TRUE)))
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
time12 weeks 
     1.12907 
\end{Soutput}
\begin{Sinput}
R> mantelhaen.test(xt)$estimate
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
common odds ratio 
          1.16667 
\end{Soutput}
\begin{Sinput}
R> ### biased ML, the default
R> exp(coef(free1way(xt, MPL_Jeffreys = FALSE)))
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
time12 weeks 
     1.36111 
\end{Soutput}
\end{Schunk}
%%
It is important to note that for null hypothesis testing only, patients or
blocks with the same outcome value at the two time points do not play a role
and are removed (as noted for McNemar's test).  In contrast, for estimating
the treatment effect, these observations contribute information and
therefore have to be retained.  In the analysis above, not only the log-odds
ratio but also all patient-specific intercepts are identical (up to their
signs, the treatment effect comes last for \code{glm} and first for
\code{free1way})
%%
<<Firth, eval = FALSE>>=
max(abs(coef(Firth)[-length(coef(Firth))] + fJ$par[-1L]))
@
%%
\begin{Schunk}
\begin{Sinput}
R> max(abs(coef(Firth)[-length(coef(Firth))] + fJ$par[-1L]))
\end{Sinput}
\begin{Soutput}
[1] 1.9237e-06
\end{Soutput}
\end{Schunk}
%%

Computing penalised estimators is slow because the penalised log-likelihood
is currently implemented but the corresponding score function is still
missing.

\section{Peculiarities} \label{app:edge}

In the framework implemented by \code{free1way}, permutation score tests are
always available and valid, because the model is only fitted under the null
hypothesis and the intercept parameters can always be estimated.  Estimating
treatment effects and intercept parameters simultaneously may however fail
because of a very flat log-likelihood as it is the case when there is no
overlap of the outcomes between the two groups.

For example, with observations $1, \dots, N$ in one group and $N + 1, \dots,
2 N$ in the other group, we can perform an exact Wilcoxon test both using
\code{wilcox.test}
%%
<<small-wt>>=
N <- 5
d <- data.frame(groups = gl(2, N))
d$y <- seq_len(2 * N)
wilcox.test(y ~ groups, data = d, exact = TRUE)$p.value
@
%%
and \code{free1way}
%%
<<small-f1w, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE>>=
f <- free1way(y ~ groups, data = d, exact = TRUE)
summary(f, test = "Permutation")$p.value
@
%%
The call to \code{free1way} produces many warnings about optimisation
problems, because the log-likelihood is flat.  This is also visible from
looking at the Hessian of the profile log-likelihood
%%
<<small-f1w-he>>=
f$hessian
@
%%
and we can vary the log-odds ratio $\delta$ while refitting the intercept
parameters by directly accessing the profile log-likelihood
%%
<<small-f1w-ll, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE>>=
coef(f)
theta <- f$par[-1L]
delta <- 20:60
logLik(f)
range(sapply(delta, function(d)
    -f$profile(c(d, theta), fix = 1L)$value))
@
%%

It might also come as a surprise that a joint analysis of all treatment
groups produces slightly different results than an analysis contrasting
subsets thereof.  The reason is that the intercept parameters apply to all
groups and thus removing or adding groups has an impact on both the
intercept and the treatment effect parameters.  In this small example,
differences in means computed by \code{lm} are not affected by the number of
groups in the model but log-odds ratios differ slightly
%%
<<addgroups>>=
d <- rfree1way(25, delta = c(.5, .75))

### identical
cbind(coef(lm(y ~ groups, data = d))[-1L],
      c(coef(lm(y ~ groups, data = d, subset = groups != "C"))[-1L],
        coef(lm(y ~ groups, data = d, subset = groups != "B"))[-1L]))

### not identical
cbind(coef(free1way(y ~ groups, data = d)),
      c(coef(free1way(y ~ groups, data = d, subset = groups != "C")),
        coef(free1way(y ~ groups, data = d, subset = groups != "B"))))
@
%%

